The docs cover this differently per framework. Since you didn't specify one, here are the options in the provided context.

## Next.js

Add a `loading.js` file next to your `page.js`. Next.js wraps the page in a `<Suspense>` boundary and uses your loading component as the fallback [1].

```tsx
// app/feed/loading.tsx
export default function Loading() {
  // Or a custom loading skeleton component
  return <p>Loading...</p>
}
```
[2]

The layout renders immediately, the loading UI shows instantly, and the page's HTML replaces it once it finishes loading [1]. The docs recommend meaningful loading states, such as skeletons and spinners [3]. The docs don't include a specific spinner component for Next.js, so you'd put your own spinner markup inside `Loading`.

## Angular

Resolvers block navigation while they run. To give feedback, read the router's current navigation state and show an indicator:

```angular-ts
import {Component, computed, inject} from '@angular/core';
import {Router, RouterOutlet} from '@angular/router';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  imports: [RouterOutlet],
  template: `
    @if (isNavigating()) {
      <div class="loading-bar">Loading...</div>
    }
    <router-outlet />
  `,
})
export class App {
  private router = inject(Router);
  isNavigating = computed(() => !!this.router.currentNavigation());
}
```
[4]

Alternatively, use non-blocking route resources to render the component immediately with skeleton or loading indicators [4].

## React Native

`ActivityIndicator` displays a circular loading indicator [5]. The provided docs don't cover how to wire it to page data loading.

Use the `useSearchParams` hook from `next/navigation`. It's a Client Component hook that returns a read-only version of the `URLSearchParams` interface. [1]

```tsx
'use client'

import { useSearchParams } from 'next/navigation'

export default function SearchBar() {
  const searchParams = useSearchParams()

  const search = searchParams.get('search')

  // URL -> `/dashboard?search=my-project`
  // `search` -> 'my-project'
  return <>Search: {search}</>
}
```
[1]

Useful read-only methods:

- `get()` returns the first value for a parameter, or `null` if it's missing. For `/dashboard?a=1&a=2`, `get("a")` returns `'1'`; use `getAll()` for all values. [4]
- `has()` returns a boolean indicating whether the parameter exists. [4]
- Others include `getAll()`, `keys()`, `values()`, `entries()`, `forEach()`, and `toString()`. [3]

**Alternative:** If the Client Component is a Page, you can read the `searchParams` prop with React's `use` function, since Client Components can't be `async`. In that case `searchParams` is a promise. [2]

```tsx
'use client'

import { use } from 'react'

export default function Page({
  searchParams,
}: {
  searchParams: Promise<{ [key: string]: string | string[] | undefined }>
}) {
  const { query } = use(searchParams)
}
```
[2]

**Notes:**
- `useSearchParams` isn't supported in Server Components. To use search params for data fetching in a Server Component, read the Page's `searchParams` prop and pass it down via props. [3]
- If your app includes the `/pages` directory, `useSearchParams` returns `ReadonlyURLSearchParams | null`. [3]

Call `redirect('/login')` from `next/navigation` inside the Server Component when the check fails. It throws a `NEXT_REDIRECT` error and stops rendering of that route segment [1].

```tsx
import { redirect } from 'next/navigation'

async function fetchTeam(id: string) {
  const res = await fetch('https://...')
  if (!res.ok) return undefined
  return res.json()
}

export default async function Profile({
  params,
}: {
  params: Promise<{ id: string }>
}) {
  const { id } = await params
  const team = await fetchTeam(id)

  if (!team) {
    redirect('/login')
  }

  // ...
}
```
[1]

You don't need `return redirect()`, because `redirect` uses the TypeScript `never` type [1].

The docs' example checks a fetched `team` value rather than a session, so substitute your own sign-in check in the `if`.

**Alternatives from the docs:**

- **Proxy:** To redirect before rendering, e.g. for all `/dashboard/:path*` routes, return `NextResponse.redirect(new URL('/login', request.url))` from `proxy.ts` when the user isn't authenticated [2].
- **`unauthorized()`:** To show a login UI at a 401 rather than redirect, call `unauthorized()` from `next/navigation`. It renders your `app/unauthorized.tsx` file [3][4][5].

Wrap the folder name in square brackets to create a dynamic segment, e.g. `app/blog/[slug]/page.js`, where `[slug]` is the dynamic segment for blog posts [2]. A URL like `/blog/my-first-post` matches `app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx` [5].

## Reading the slug

Dynamic segments are passed as the `params` prop to `layout`, `page`, `route`, and `generateMetadata` functions [2]. In a page, `params` is a Promise, so `await` it and destructure `slug`:

```tsx
// app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx
export default async function Page({
  params,
}: {
  params: Promise<{ slug: string }>
}) {
  const { slug } = await params
  return <h1>Blog Post: {slug}</h1>
}
```
[1]

For `/blog/a`, `params` is `{ slug: 'a' }`, and likewise for `/blog/b` and `/blog/c` [2].

## Fetching content with the slug

You can use the slug to fetch specific content for the page [1][3]:

```tsx
export default async function BlogPostPage({
  params,
}: {
  params: Promise<{ slug: string }>
}) {
  const { slug } = await params
  const post = await getPost(slug)

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>{post.title}</h1>
      <p>{post.content}</p>
    </div>
  )
}
```
[3]

(`getPost` here is your own data-fetching function; the docs don't define it.)

## Other segment forms

- `[segment]` matches a single param.
- `[...segment]` is a catch-all.
- `[[...segment]]` is an optional catch-all. [5]

Yes. Supply the full list of paths from `generateStaticParams` and Next.js will statically render all of them at build time [1]:

```tsx
// app/blog/[slug]/page.tsx
export async function generateStaticParams() {
  const posts = await fetch('https://.../posts').then((res) => res.json())

  return posts.map((post) => ({
    slug: post.slug,
  }))
}
```
[1]

A few related points:

- You must always return an array from `generateStaticParams`, even if it's empty. Otherwise, the route is rendered dynamically [1].
- With ISR, a page is prerendered for each returned post during `next build` [2].
- Prerendering every page increases build work and produces output that has to be stored and deployed. The docs suggest prerendering only the routes that benefit most, such as popular pages, and generating the rest on demand [3]. To do that, return a partial list, for example `posts.slice(0, 10)` [1].

Export a `Metadata` object from each route's `page.js` (or `layout.js`) with its own `title` and `description`. [2][3]

```tsx
// app/blog/page.tsx
import type { Metadata } from 'next'

export const metadata: Metadata = {
  title: 'My Blog',
  description: '...',
}

export default function Page() {}
```
[2][3]

A `description` renders as a `<meta name="description">` tag in the `<head>`. [4]

```html
<meta name="description" content="The React Framework for the Web" />
```
[4]

## Sharing a title pattern across pages

If you want a consistent suffix or prefix on every page title, define a `title.template` in a parent layout. A `default` is required with a template. [1]

```tsx
// app/layout.tsx
export const metadata: Metadata = {
  title: {
    template: '%s | Acme',
    default: 'Acme',
  },
}
```
[1]

```tsx
// app/about/page.tsx
export const metadata: Metadata = {
  title: 'About',
}

// Output: <title>About | Acme</title>
```
[1]

Notes:
- A template applies only to child segments, not to the segment where it's defined. [1]
- To skip the parent's template for a page, use `title.absolute`. [5]
- If a page doesn't define its own title, the closest parent's resolved title is used. [5]

## Not covered

The docs provided only cover static metadata via the `metadata` object. They don't show how to set titles or descriptions dynamically per page, for example based on route params.

Call the async `cookies()` function from `next/headers` with `await`, then use `.get('name')` on the result:

```tsx
import { cookies } from 'next/headers'

export default async function Page() {
  const cookieStore = await cookies()
  const theme = cookieStore.get('theme')
  return '...'
}
```
[1][2]

To check whether a cookie exists, use `.has(name)`:

```tsx
const cookieStore = await cookies()
const hasCookie = cookieStore.has('theme')
```
[4]

Reading works in Server Components because the browser sends cookie data to the server in the request headers. Setting or deleting cookies (`.set`, `.delete`) isn't supported during Server Component rendering. Do that in a Server Function or Route Handler instead. [3]

Create a Route Handler with the `route.ts` (or `route.js`) file convention inside the `app/` folder. Route Handlers are public HTTP endpoints, and the file `app/api/route.ts` handles requests to `/api`. [4]

## Minimal JSON endpoint

Route Handlers use the Web `Request` and `Response` APIs, so you can return JSON with `Response.json`: [2]

```ts
// app/api/route.ts
export async function GET() {
  return Response.json({ message: 'Hello World' })
}
```

## Using `NextResponse`

You can also use `NextResponse.json()` from `next/server`, which produces a response with the given JSON body. The second argument sets options such as the status code: [1]

```ts
// app/api/route.ts
import { NextResponse } from 'next/server'

export async function GET(request: Request) {
  return NextResponse.json({ error: 'Internal Server Error' }, { status: 500 })
}
```

## Handling errors

Use `try/catch` around operations that may throw, and avoid exposing sensitive information in error messages sent to the client: [4]

```ts
// app/api/route.ts
import { submit } from '@/lib/submit'

export async function POST(request: Request) {
  try {
    await submit(request)
    return new Response(null, { status: 204 })
  } catch (reason) {
    const message =
      reason instanceof Error ? reason.message : 'Unexpected error'

    return new Response(message, { status: 500 })
  }
}
```

## Notes

- Since Route Handlers are public, any client can access them. To restrict access, implement authentication and authorization. [4]
- For a new project, `create-next-app` with the `--api` flag includes an example `route.ts` in the `app/` folder (e.g. `npx create-next-app@latest --api`). [3]

The answer depends on your framework. The docs provided cover Next.js and Angular.

## Next.js

**Route-segment level:** Add an `error.tsx` file to a route segment and export a Client Component. It wraps that segment and its nested children in an error boundary and shows the fallback UI when something throws [2][4].

```tsx
'use client' // Error boundaries must be Client Components

import { useEffect } from 'react'

export default function ErrorPage({
  error,
  retry,
}: {
  error: unknown
  retry: () => void
}) {
  useEffect(() => {
    console.error(error)
  }, [error])

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Something went wrong!</h2>
      <button onClick={() => retry()}>Try again</button>
    </div>
  )
}
```

Errors bubble up to the nearest parent error boundary, so you can place `error.tsx` files at different levels of the route hierarchy [2]. `error.js` doesn't wrap the `layout.js` or `template.js` in the same segment. For root layout errors, use `global-error.js` [4].

**Component level:** Use `catchError` to create a boundary that can wrap any part of the tree [2]:

```tsx
'use client'

import { catchError, type ErrorInfo } from 'next/error'

function ErrorFallback(props: { title: string }, { error, retry }: ErrorInfo) {
  const message = error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error)

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>{props.title}</h2>
      <p>{message}</p>
      <button onClick={() => retry()}>Try again</button>
    </div>
  )
}

export default catchError(ErrorFallback)
```

Then wrap part of your UI with the returned component [1][3]:

```tsx
<ErrorWrapper title="Dashboard Error">{children}</ErrorWrapper>
```

**Caveat:** Error boundaries only catch errors during rendering, not in event handlers or async code. For those, catch the error yourself and store it with `useState` or `useReducer`, then render a fallback [1]. Unhandled errors inside `startTransition` do bubble up to the nearest error boundary [1].

## Angular

Wrap the risky part of your template in `@boundary`. If a component or directive inside throws during initialization or change detection, Angular renders the `@error` block instead [5].

```angular-html
@boundary {
  <app-risky-component />
} @error {
  <p>Something went wrong!</p>
}
```

Call a cache-invalidation function inside the Server Action after the mutation. Which one depends on how your list data is cached.

## Invalidate by path: `revalidatePath`

```ts
'use server'

import { revalidatePath } from 'next/cache'
import { auth } from '@/lib/auth'
import { db } from '@/lib/db'

export async function createPost(formData: FormData) {
  const session = await auth()
  if (!session?.user) throw new Error('Unauthorized')

  await db.post.create({
    data: {
      title: String(formData.get('title')),
      authorId: session.user.id,
    },
  })

  revalidatePath('/posts')
}
```
[2][3]

## Invalidate by tag: `updateTag`

If your list data is tagged, use `updateTag` so the new post is immediately visible. You can also redirect to the new post afterward:

```ts
'use server'

import { updateTag } from 'next/cache'
import { redirect } from 'next/navigation'

export async function createPost(formData: FormData) {
  const title = formData.get('title')
  const content = formData.get('content')

  const post = await db.post.create({
    data: { title, content },
  })

  updateTag('posts')
  updateTag(`post-${post.id}`)

  redirect(`/posts/${post.id}`)
}
```
[1]

## Refresh the current page: `refresh`

If you just want to refresh the current route, call `refresh()` from `next/cache`:

```ts
'use server'

import { refresh } from 'next/cache'

export async function createPost(formData: FormData) {
  // ...create the post
  refresh()
}
```
[4][5]

`refresh()` does not revalidate tagged data. For tagged data, use `updateTag` or `revalidateTag` instead. [5] It also only works inside Server Actions, and calling it from a route handler throws an error. [4]

## No follow-up fetch needed

When the action calls `updateTag`, `revalidatePath`, `refresh`, or `redirect`, or mutates cookies, Next.js re-renders the current route in the same HTTP response as the action. Your code doesn't need a separate fetch to see the updated UI. [2]

The docs don't name a single "best" way. It depends on your framework and deployment.

## Next.js

Use the `next/image` component. Next.js optimizes images on demand, serves modern formats like WebP, and handles lazy loading and responsive sizing [5].

- **Self-hosted with `next start`:** it works with zero configuration [3].
- **Static export (`output: 'export'`):** define a custom image loader in `next.config.js` [1]:

  ```js
  /** @type {import('next').NextConfig} */
  const nextConfig = {
    output: 'export',
    images: {
      loader: 'custom',
      loaderFile: './my-loader.ts',
    },
  }

  module.exports = nextConfig
  ```
  [1]

  The loader builds the image URL, for example for Cloudinary [1]:

  ```ts
  export default function cloudinaryLoader({
    src,
    width,
    quality,
  }: {
    src: string
    width: number
    quality?: number
  }) {
    const params = ['f_auto', 'c_limit', `w_${width}`, `q_${quality || 'auto'}`]
    return `https://res.cloudinary.com/demo/image/upload/${params.join(
      ','
    )}${src}`
  }
  ```
  [1]

  Then use the component as usual:

  ```tsx
  import Image from 'next/image'

  export default function Page() {
    return <Image alt="turtles" src="/turtles.jpg" width={300} height={300} />
  }
  ```
  [1]

- **Cloud provider instead of the built-in optimizer:** use the same `loader: 'custom'` and `loaderFile` config. The file must export a default function that returns a string [4]. You can also pass a `loader` prop to individual `next/image` instances [4].

## Angular

Use Angular's built-in image directive, `NgOptimizedImage`, which generates `srcset`s automatically [2].

The docs provided don't cover image optimization for React Native or Jest.

Put it in a **Proxy** file: `proxy.ts` (or `.js`) in the project root, or inside `src` if you use one, at the same level as `app` or `pages`. Proxy runs on the server before a request is completed and before routes are rendered, and can rewrite, redirect, modify headers, or respond directly [3]. If you've customized `pageExtensions`, name the file accordingly, e.g. `proxy.page.ts` [3].

> **Note:** The `middleware` file convention is deprecated and renamed to `proxy` [3].

## Auth redirect example

```ts
// proxy.ts
import { NextResponse, NextRequest } from 'next/server'
import { authenticate } from 'auth-provider'

export function proxy(request: NextRequest) {
  const isAuthenticated = authenticate(request)

  if (isAuthenticated) {
    return NextResponse.next()
  }

  return NextResponse.redirect(new URL('/login', request.url))
}

export const config = {
  matcher: '/dashboard/:path*',
}
```
[1]

## Applying it to many routes

- Proxy is invoked for **every route in your project**, so use `matcher` to target or exclude specific routes [5].
- Because it runs on every route, it's a good place to centralize redirect logic and pre-filter unauthorized users [2].
- Only read the session from the cookie (optimistic checks) and avoid database checks, since Proxy also runs on prefetched routes [2].

A broad matcher that excludes API and static assets looks like this [2]:

```ts
export const config = {
  matcher: ['/((?!api|_next/static|_next/image|.*\\.png$).*)'],
}
```

## Things to keep in mind

- Proxy runs after `redirects` in `next.config.js` and before rendering [1][5].
- Proxy is meant to run separately from your render code, so don't rely on shared modules or globals. To pass information to your app, use headers, cookies, rewrites, redirects, or the URL [3].
- Server Functions are handled as POST requests to the route where they're used, so a matcher that excludes a path also skips Server Function calls there. Verify authentication and authorization inside each Server Function rather than relying on Proxy alone [5].

Create a mock with `jest.fn()`, run the code under test, then assert on the arguments using matchers or the mock's `mock` property.

## Matchers

**Any call:** `.toHaveBeenCalledWith` checks that the mock was called with specific arguments, using the same algorithm as `.toEqual`. [3]

```js
test('registration applies correctly to orange La Croix', () => {
  const beverage = new LaCroix('orange');
  register(beverage);
  const f = jest.fn();
  applyToAll(f);
  expect(f).toHaveBeenCalledWith(beverage);
});
```
[3]

**Specific call number:** `.toHaveBeenNthCalledWith(nthCall, ...)` checks the arguments of the nth call. The number must be a positive integer starting from 1. [4]

```js
test('drinkEach drinks each drink', () => {
  const drink = jest.fn();
  drinkEach(drink, ['lemon', 'octopus']);
  expect(drink).toHaveBeenNthCalledWith(1, 'lemon');
  expect(drink).toHaveBeenNthCalledWith(2, 'octopus');
});
```
[4]

**Last call:** `.toHaveBeenLastCalledWith` checks the arguments of the most recent call. [5]

```js
test('applying to all flavors does mango last', () => {
  const drink = jest.fn();
  applyToAllFlavors(drink);
  expect(drink).toHaveBeenLastCalledWith('mango');
});
```
[5]

## Inspecting arguments directly

- `mockFn.mock.calls` is an array with one entry per call, and each entry is the array of arguments for that call. For calls `f('arg1', 'arg2')` and `f('arg3', 'arg4')`, it is `[['arg1', 'arg2'], ['arg3', 'arg4']]`. [1]
- `mockFn.mock.lastCall` is the arguments of the last call (`['arg3', 'arg4']` in the example above), or `undefined` if the mock was never called. [2]

Call `jest.mock('axios')` to automatically mock the whole module, then set return values on its methods with `mockResolvedValue`. Here is the example from the docs, using a `Users` class that calls `axios.get`:

```js
import axios from 'axios';
import Users from './users';

jest.mock('axios');

test('should fetch users', () => {
  const users = [{name: 'Bob'}];
  const resp = {data: users};
  axios.get.mockResolvedValue(resp);

  // or you could use the following depending on your use case:
  // axios.get.mockImplementation(() => Promise.resolve(resp))

  return Users.all().then(data => expect(data).toEqual(users));
});
```
[1]

This makes `axios.get('/users.json')` return a fake response, so the test doesn't hit the real API [1].

A few related notes:

- **Scope:** A module mocked with `jest.mock` is mocked only in the file that calls it. Other files importing the module get the original implementation [2].
- **Different mocks in the same file:** `jest.mock` calls are hoisted to the top of the code block when using `babel-jest`. If you need to mock a module differently from test to test, use `jest.doMock()` (combined with `jest.resetModules()` and `require`) to avoid the hoisting [3].
- **Setup files:** Importing a module in a setup file (via `setupFilesAfterEnv`) prevents mocking for that module and all modules it imports [2].
- **TypeScript:** Use the `jest.Mocked` utility type or the `jest.mocked()` helper to get typed mocked modules [2].

You can test it in a few ways. In every case, Jest has to be told to wait for the promise.

## Return the promise

Return a promise from the test, and Jest waits for it to resolve. If the promise is rejected, the test fails. [2]

```js
test('the data is peanut butter', () => {
  return fetchData().then(data => {
    expect(data).toBe('peanut butter');
  });
});
```
[2]

## async/await

Put `async` in front of the test function and `await` the call. [3]

```js
test('the data is peanut butter', async () => {
  const data = await fetchData();
  expect(data).toBe('peanut butter');
});
```
[3]

## `.resolves` / `.rejects`

These unwrap the promise so you can chain any other matcher. You can combine them with `async`/`await`. [3][4]

```js
test('the data is peanut butter', async () => {
  await expect(fetchData()).resolves.toBe('peanut butter');
});

test('the fetch fails with an error', async () => {
  await expect(fetchData()).rejects.toMatch('error');
});
```
[3]

Without `async`/`await`, return the unwrapped assertion instead: `return expect(promise).resolves.toBe(...)`. [4]

## Testing rejections

If you expect a rejection, use `.catch` and add `expect.assertions`. Otherwise a fulfilled promise would not fail the test. [3]

```js
test('the fetch fails with an error', () => {
  expect.assertions(1);
  return fetchData().catch(error => expect(error).toMatch('error'));
});
```
[3]

## Common pitfall

Always `return` or `await` the promise. If you omit it, the test completes before the promise resolves or rejects. [3]

Use `beforeEach(fn, timeout)`. It runs a function before each test in the file, which makes it a good place to reset global state. If the function returns a promise (or is a generator), Jest waits for it to resolve before running the test. [1]

```js
const globalDatabase = makeGlobalDatabase();

beforeEach(() => {
  // Clears the database and adds some testing data.
  // Jest will wait for this promise to resolve before running tests.
  return globalDatabase.clear().then(() => {
    return globalDatabase.insert({testData: 'foo'});
  });
});
```
[1]

Notes:
- You can pass an optional `timeout` in milliseconds as a second argument. The default is 5 seconds. [1]
- If `beforeEach` is inside a `describe` block, it runs only for the tests in that block. [1]
- If you only need setup to run once before any tests, use `beforeAll` instead. [1]

**Resetting mocks or modules specifically**

- Mocks: set the `resetMocks` config option (or the `--resetMocks` CLI flag) to automatically call `jest.resetAllMocks()` before each test. This removes fake implementations but doesn't restore the initial ones. [2][3]
- Module registry: call `jest.resetModules()` in a `beforeEach`, or enable the `resetModules` config option to do it before every test. [4][5]

```js
beforeEach(() => {
  jest.resetModules();
});
```
[4]

Yes. Jest supports TypeScript in a few ways.

## Transpiling TypeScript

**Via Babel:** Install `@babel/preset-typescript` and add it to the presets in `babel.config.js` (after following the Babel setup instructions) [3].

```javascript
module.exports = {
  presets: [
    ['@babel/preset-env', {targets: {node: 'current'}}],
    '@babel/preset-typescript',
  ],
};
```

Babel only transpiles, so Jest won't type-check your tests when they run. If you want type-checking, use `ts-jest` instead, or run `tsc` separately (or as part of your build) [3].

**Via `ts-jest`:** Install it with `npm install --save-dev ts-jest`. You may also need to create a configuration file for it [3].

## Typing the Jest globals

There are two options [3]:

1. **`@jest/globals`**: Its type definitions ship with Jest and are updated whenever Jest is. Install it and import the APIs [1][3]:

   ```ts
   import {describe, expect, test} from '@jest/globals';
   import {sum} from './sum';

   describe('sum module', () => {
     test('adds 1 + 2 to equal 3', () => {
       expect(sum(1, 2)).toBe(3);
     });
   });
   ```

2. **`@types/jest`**: This provides types for the Jest globals without imports. It's a third-party package maintained at DefinitelyTyped, so it may not cover the latest Jest features. Match its version to your Jest version as closely as possible, for example `27.4.x` for Jest `27.4.0` [1].

The TypeScript examples in the Jest Globals docs only work as documented if you explicitly import the Jest APIs [2][4].

Use `jest.spyOn(object, methodName)`. It tracks calls to `object[methodName]`, and by default it also calls the original method, so the behavior isn't replaced. [1]

```js
const video = require('./video');

afterEach(() => {
  // restore the spy created with spyOn
  jest.restoreAllMocks();
});

test('plays video', () => {
  const spy = jest.spyOn(video, 'play');
  const isPlaying = video.play();

  expect(spy).toHaveBeenCalled();
  expect(isPlaying).toBe(true);
});
```
[1]

Here `video.play()` still returns its real value (`true`), and the spy records the call. [1]

Some related points:

- **Restoring:** Calling `jest.restoreAllMocks()` in an `afterEach` hook restores the original state. [1]
- **Automatic restore with `using`:** If your codebase transpiles explicit resource management (for example TypeScript >= 5.2), `using spy = jest.spyOn(...)` restores the spy automatically when the block is left. [1][4]
- **Getters and setters:** Pass `'get'` or `'set'` as the third argument, e.g. `jest.spyOn(video, 'play', 'get')`. [2]
- **Overriding instead:** To replace the original, chain `.mockImplementation(() => customImplementation)`. [1]

Jest can mock ES6 classes because they are constructor functions with syntactic sugar, so a mock must be a function or an actual ES6 class. You can build it with mock functions [1]. The docs list four ways to create a class mock, and the context covers two of them.

## Module factory with `jest.mock()`

Pass a module factory, a function that returns the mock. To mock a constructor, the factory must return a constructor function [4].

```javascript
import SoundPlayer from './sound-player';
const mockPlaySoundFile = jest.fn();
jest.mock('./sound-player', () => {
  return jest.fn().mockImplementation(() => {
    return {playSoundFile: mockPlaySoundFile};
  });
});
```
[4]

Because `jest.mock()` calls are hoisted, Jest blocks access to out-of-scope variables unless their names start with `mock`. You still have to make sure they are initialized in time. For example, a variable named `fakePlaySoundFile` fails, and a `mockSoundPlayer` defined outside the factory and not wrapped in an arrow function throws a `ReferenceError` [4].

### Spying on the constructor

Using `jest.fn().mockImplementation()` lets you check constructor calls, for example `expect(SoundPlayer).toHaveBeenCalled();` or `SoundPlayer.mock.calls` [3].

### Non-default exports

If the class isn't the default export, return an object keyed by the export name [3]:

```javascript
import {SoundPlayer} from './sound-player';
jest.mock('./sound-player', () => {
  return {
    SoundPlayer: jest.fn().mockImplementation(() => {
      return {playSoundFile: () => {}};
    }),
  };
});
```
[3]

## Manual mock in `__mocks__`

Create a file with the same name as the mocked module in a `__mocks__` folder, containing an ES6 class. It replaces the real class, but you can't spy on calls with it [5].

```javascript
// __mocks__/sound-player.js
export default class SoundPlayer {
  constructor() {
    console.log('Mock SoundPlayer: constructor was called');
  }

  playSoundFile() {
    console.log('Mock SoundPlayer: playSoundFile was called');
  }
}
```
[5]

The docs provided don't cover the other two of the four ways to create a class mock.

Use the `jsdom` test environment. Jest's default is `node`, and `jsdom` emulates a browser by providing browser APIs such as `document` and `window` [1][5].

**1. Install the environment package** (if it isn't already installed):

```bash
npm install --save-dev jest-environment-jsdom
```
[2]

**2. Enable it** with the `testEnvironment` option, which defaults to `node` [5]. The docs show the per-file docblock form:

```js
/**
 * @jest-environment jsdom
 */

test('use jsdom in this test file', () => {
  const element = document.createElement('div');
  expect(element).not.toBeNull();
});
```
[5]

The docblock applies to all tests in that file [5].

**Optional: configure the environment.** You can pass options via `testEnvironmentOptions`, or in a docblock with `@jest-environment-options` (the value must be parseable by `JSON.parse`) [3]:

```js
/**
 * @jest-environment jsdom
 * @jest-environment-options {"url": "https://jestjs.io/"}
 */

test('use jsdom and set the URL in this test file', () => {
  expect(window.location.href).toBe('https://jestjs.io/');
});
```
[3]

**Caveat:** jsdom doesn't implement every browser API. For example, `window.matchMedia()` throws `TypeError: window.matchMedia is not a function`, so you need to mock it [4].

The docs provided don't show the syntax for setting `testEnvironment` globally in a Jest config file, only the per-file docblock and the option's description.

## Setup

**1. Install the testing library.** `@testing-library/react-native` replaces the deprecated `react-test-renderer`, and needs `test-renderer` installed alongside it as a peer dependency [1]:

```bash
yarn add --dev @testing-library/react-native test-renderer
```

**2. Write the test.** Render the component and pass the output of `screen.toJSON()` to `toMatchSnapshot()` [1]:

```tsx
// __tests__/Intro-test.js
import {render, screen} from '@testing-library/react-native';
import React from 'react';
import Intro from '../Intro';

test('renders correctly', async () => {
  await render(<Intro />);
  expect(screen.toJSON()).toMatchSnapshot();
});
```

**3. Run the tests.** Running `yarn test` or `jest` produces a snapshot file, for example `__tests__/__snapshots__/Intro-test.js.snap` [2].

## Workflow

- On later runs, Jest compares the rendered output to the saved snapshot. Commit the snapshot with your code changes [2].
- If a snapshot test fails, check whether the change was intended. If it was, run `jest -u` to overwrite the existing snapshot [2].
- Snapshots are considered correct when created, even if the rendered output is wrong. Large snapshots are also hard to review, and it's tempting to update a failing snapshot without investigating it, so review changes carefully [4].

The docs' full example is at [examples/react-native](https://github.com/jestjs/jest/tree/main/examples/react-native) [2].

The provided docs don't cover any additional Jest configuration for React Native, such as presets.

In React Native, use the `BackHandler` API (Android-only). It lets you register listeners for the hardware back press and control how your app responds. [3]

## Register a listener

`BackHandler.addEventListener` returns an `EventSubscription`, which you should clear with `remove()`. This example confirms before exiting the app:

```jsx
import {useEffect} from 'react';
import {BackHandler, Alert} from 'react-native';

const App = () => {
  useEffect(() => {
    const backAction = () => {
      Alert.alert('Hold on!', 'Are you sure you want to go back?', [
        {
          text: 'Cancel',
          onPress: () => null,
          style: 'cancel',
        },
        {text: 'YES', onPress: () => BackHandler.exitApp()},
      ]);
      return true;
    };

    const backHandler = BackHandler.addEventListener(
      'hardwareBackPress',
      backAction,
    );

    return () => backHandler.remove();
  }, []);

  // ...
};
```
[5]

## How handlers are called

- Subscriptions are called in reverse order, so the last registered one runs first. [3]
- If a subscription returns `true`, earlier-registered subscriptions are not called. [3]
- If none return `true` (or none are registered), the default back behavior runs and exits the app. [3]
- If a `Modal` is open, `BackHandler` publishes no events. [3]

## Other cases

- **React Navigation:** follow their [Custom Android back button behaviour](https://reactnavigation.org/docs/custom-android-back-button-handling/) guide. [2]
- **Embedding React Native in a native Android Fragment:** your host Activity isn't a `ReactActivity`, so it must implement `DefaultHardwareBackBtnHandler` and delegate to the `OnBackPressedDispatcher`. [4]

```kotlin
class MainActivity : AppCompatActivity(), DefaultHardwareBackBtnHandler {
    // ...
    override fun invokeDefaultOnBackPressed() {
        onBackPressedDispatcher.onBackPressed()
    }
}
```
[4]

Wrap the content that the keyboard might cover in a `KeyboardAvoidingView`. The docs recommend it for interactive elements that can get covered by the software keyboard, which can take almost half the screen. [1]

Here's the docs' example, which uses `behavior="padding"`. The Sign Up button and text adjust their positions so they aren't hidden under the keyboard: [3][5]

```tsx
import {useState, useRef} from 'react';
import {
  Alert,
  Text,
  Button,
  TextInput,
  KeyboardAvoidingView,
  View,
} from 'react-native';

const App = () => {
  const emailInput = useRef(null);
  const [email, setEmail] = useState('');

  const submit = () => {
    emailInput.current.blur();
    Alert.alert(`Confirmation email has been sent to ${email}`);
  };

  return (
    <View style={{flex: 1}}>
      <KeyboardAvoidingView behavior="padding">
        <TextInput
          value={email}
          onChangeText={text => setEmail(text)}
          ref={emailInput}
          placeholder="email@example.com"
          keyboardType="email-address"
          returnKeyType="send"
          onSubmitEditing={submit}
          blurOnSubmit={true}
        />
        <View>
          <Button title="Sign Up" onPress={submit} />
        </View>
      </KeyboardAvoidingView>
    </View>
  );
};
```

This is a trimmed version of the docs' example (styles omitted). [3][5]

**Alternative for a sticky input:** On iOS, `InputAccessoryView` can anchor a `TextInput` to the top of the keyboard if you wrap the input with it and don't set a `nativeID`. However, this component is deprecated and will be removed in a future release. [2][4]

The docs provided don't cover other `KeyboardAvoidingView` props or `behavior` values beyond `"padding"`.

React Native gives you two ways to separate code by platform [3].

## 1. `Platform` module (for small differences)

Use `Platform.OS`, which is `ios` on iOS and `android` on Android [1]:

```tsx
import {Platform, StyleSheet} from 'react-native';

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  height: Platform.OS === 'ios' ? 200 : 100,
});
```

You can also use `Platform.select`. It takes an object with keys `'ios' | 'android' | 'native' | 'default'` and returns the best match for the current platform. `ios` and `android` take preference on a phone, then `native`, then `default` [1].

```tsx
import {Platform, StyleSheet} from 'react-native';

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  container: {
    flex: 1,
    ...Platform.select({
      ios: {
        backgroundColor: 'red',
      },
      android: {
        backgroundColor: 'green',
      },
      default: {
        // other platforms, web for example
        backgroundColor: 'blue',
      },
    }),
  },
});
```

`Platform.select` accepts any value, so it can also return platform-specific components [1]:

```tsx
const Component = Platform.select({
  ios: () => require('ComponentIOS'),
  android: () => require('ComponentAndroid'),
})();

<Component />;
```

On Android, `Platform.Version` gives the Android API version (not the OS version) [4].

## 2. Platform-specific file extensions (for larger differences)

For more complex code, split it into separate files with `.ios.` or `.android.` extensions [2]:

```shell
BigButton.ios.js
BigButton.android.js
```

Then import without the extension, and React Native loads the right file for the running platform [2]:

```tsx
import BigButton from './BigButton';
```

The docs cover this for both Angular and React Native, so here are both.

## Angular

Use `HttpClient`, which has a method for each HTTP verb. Each method returns an RxJS `Observable` that sends the request when you subscribe and emits the result when the server responds. [2]

```ts
http.get<Config>('/api/config').subscribe((config) => {
  // process the configuration.
});
```
[3]

- `get()` takes the endpoint URL and an optional options object. [3]
- The generic type (`Config`) is optional. If you omit it, the data is typed as `Object`. [3]
- The generic is only a type assertion, and `HttpClient` doesn't check that the response matches it. [3]
- Each subscription to the `Observable` makes a new backend request. [2]

The provided docs don't cover how to set up `HttpClient`. The passage on that only has a heading. [1]

## React Native

Use the built-in Fetch API. Pass the URL to `fetch`:

```ts
fetch('https://mywebsite.com/mydata.json');
```
[4]

For a POST request or custom headers, pass an options object as the second argument:

```ts
fetch('https://mywebsite.com/endpoint/', {
  method: 'POST',
  headers: {
    Accept: 'application/json',
    'Content-Type': 'application/json',
  },
  body: JSON.stringify({
    firstParam: 'yourValue',
    secondParam: 'yourOtherValue',
  }),
});
```
[4]

The docs provided don't cover handling the response in React Native.

Use the `useWindowDimensions` hook. It automatically updates its values when the screen size or font scale changes, so your component re-renders on rotation. [1]

```tsx
import {useWindowDimensions} from 'react-native';

const {height, width} = useWindowDimensions();
```
[1]

`width` is the width in pixels of the window or screen your app occupies. [3] The docs call this the preferred API for React components, because unlike `Dimensions`, it updates as the window's dimensions update. [2]

Use the values directly in render, for example in inline styles:

```tsx
<View style={{width: width / 2}} />
```

This follows the docs' guidance that logic or styles depending on dimensions should be recomputed on every render rather than cached (for example, in a `StyleSheet`). [2]

**Alternative: `Dimensions` with a listener**

If you can't use a hook, `Dimensions.get('window')` gives the width, but it doesn't update on its own. Subscribe to the `change` event to catch rotation or foldable changes: [2][5]

```tsx
const subscription = Dimensions.addEventListener(
  'change',
  ({window, screen}) => {
    setDimensions({window, screen});
  },
);
// cleanup
subscription?.remove();
```
[5]

Use `Alert.alert` from `react-native` and pass a buttons array. Each button has a `text` and an `onPress` callback, and the Cancel button can use `style: 'cancel'`:

```jsx
import {Button, Alert} from 'react-native';

const createTwoButtonAlert = () =>
  Alert.alert('Alert Title', 'My Alert Msg', [
    {
      text: 'Cancel',
      onPress: () => console.log('Cancel Pressed'),
      style: 'cancel',
    },
    {text: 'OK', onPress: () => console.log('OK Pressed')},
  ]);

<Button title={'2-Button Alert'} onPress={createTwoButtonAlert} />;
```
[1]

Tapping any button fires its `onPress` callback and dismisses the alert. [3] `Alert` works on both Android and iOS. [3]

**Android notes:**
- With two buttons, Android treats them as 'negative' and 'positive' (such as 'Cancel', 'OK'). [2]
- By default, tapping outside the alert doesn't dismiss it. To allow that, pass a fourth argument `{cancelable: true}`, and optionally handle it with `onDismiss`. [2]

```jsx
Alert.alert(
  'Alert Title',
  'My Alert Msg',
  [{text: 'Cancel', onPress: () => console.log('Cancel Pressed'), style: 'cancel'}],
  {
    cancelable: true,
    onDismiss: () => console.log('Dismissed by tapping outside'),
  },
);
```
[2]

Don't use `AlertIOS`; the docs say to use `Alert` instead. [4]

Use `Linking.openURL()` from React Native with a full `http://` or `https://` URL. It tries to open the URL with any installed app, and an `https`/`http` URL opens in the web browser on both iOS and Android [1][2].

```tsx
import {Linking} from 'react-native';

await Linking.openURL('https://google.com');
```

**Things to know:**

- The protocol (`"http://"` or `"https://"`) must be included in the URL for web links [1].
- `openURL()` returns a `Promise`. It resolves if the user confirms the open dialog or the URL opens automatically. It rejects if the user cancels or no registered app can handle the URL [1].
- For non-http(s) URLs, check `canOpenURL()` first, since `openURL()` fails if the system doesn't know how to open the URL [1].

**Example with a check and fallback** (the docs' pattern, wrapped in a button) [4][5]:

```tsx
import {useCallback} from 'react';
import {Alert, Button, Linking} from 'react-native';

const OpenURLButton = ({url, children}: {url: string; children: string}) => {
  const handlePress = useCallback(async () => {
    const supported = await Linking.canOpenURL(url);

    if (supported) {
      await Linking.openURL(url);
    } else {
      Alert.alert(`Don't know how to open this URL: ${url}`);
    }
  }, [url]);

  return <Button title={children} onPress={handlePress} />;
};
```

**Android manifest:** to handle `https` with `canOpenURL()`, the docs show adding this to your manifest [3]:

```xml
<manifest ...>
  <queries>
    <intent>
      <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
      <data android:scheme="https"/>
    </intent>
  </queries>
</manifest>
```

Use `Pressable` and pass a function to its `style` prop. The function receives a boolean `pressed` and returns view styles. [1]

