The `children` prop also accepts a function that receives `pressed`, so you can change the content (such as the text) while it's pressed. [5]

The built-in `Button` component only supports minimal customization. If it doesn't fit your app, the docs suggest building your own button with `Pressable`. [3]

Generating a signed release build has two parts: configuring signing, then building the AAB.

## 1. Add the signing config

Edit `android/app/build.gradle` so release builds are signed with your upload key [1]:

```groovy
...
android {
    ...
    defaultConfig { ... }
    signingConfigs {
        release {
            if (project.hasProperty('MYAPP_UPLOAD_STORE_FILE')) {
                storeFile file(MYAPP_UPLOAD_STORE_FILE)
                storePassword MYAPP_UPLOAD_STORE_PASSWORD
                keyAlias MYAPP_UPLOAD_KEY_ALIAS
                keyPassword MYAPP_UPLOAD_KEY_PASSWORD
            }
        }
    }
    buildTypes {
        release {
            ...
            signingConfig signingConfigs.release
        }
    }
}
...
```
[1]

Android requires apps to be signed before installation. Before an app binary is uploaded to Google Play, it must be signed with an upload key [5].

## 2. Build the release AAB

```shell
npx react-native build-android --mode=release
```
[2]

This uses Gradle's `bundleRelease` to bundle the JavaScript into the AAB. Make sure `gradle.properties` does not include `org.gradle.configureondemand=true`, because that makes the release build skip bundling JS and assets [2].

The output is at `android/app/build/outputs/bundle/release/app-release.aab`, ready to upload to Google Play [2].

## 3. Requirements and testing

- Google Play only accepts the AAB format if App Signing by Google Play is configured in the Google Play Console [2].
- To test the release build before uploading, uninstall any previous version of the app, then run `npm run android -- --mode="release"` or `yarn android --mode release`. The `--mode release` flag is only available if signing is set up as described above [4].

## Not covered

The provided docs don't show how to generate the upload key or define the `MYAPP_UPLOAD_*` properties that the Gradle config references.

In React Native, use the `Animated` API: bind an `Animated.Value` to a view's opacity and animate it from 0 to 1 with `Animated.timing`.

```tsx
import {useEffect, useRef} from 'react';
import {Animated, Text, View} from 'react-native';

const FadeInView = props => {
  const fadeAnim = useRef(new Animated.Value(0)).current; // Initial value for opacity: 0

  useEffect(() => {
    Animated.timing(fadeAnim, {
      toValue: 1,
      duration: 10000,
      useNativeDriver: true,
    }).start();
  }, [fadeAnim]);

  return (
    <Animated.View // Special animatable View
      style={{
        ...props.style,
        opacity: fadeAnim, // Bind opacity to animated value
      }}>
      {props.children}
    </Animated.View>
  );
};
```
[3]

How it works:
- `fadeAnim` is created with `useRef` and starts at 0. The `Animated.View`'s `opacity` is mapped to it. [1]
- On mount, an easing animation runs `fadeAnim` to 1, updating the dependent opacity on each frame. [1]
- You can then use `FadeInView` in place of a `View`. [3]

The `Animated` API exports animatable `View`, `Text`, `Image`, `ScrollView`, `FlatList` and `SectionList`. You can create your own with `Animated.createAnimatedComponent()`. [3]

To trigger fades on demand (e.g. from buttons), call `Animated.timing` from handlers, animating `toValue` to 1 for fade in or 0 for fade out. The docs example uses `useAnimatedValue(0)` to create the value. [2]

```tsx
const fadeAnim = useAnimatedValue(0);

const fadeIn = () => {
  Animated.timing(fadeAnim, {
    toValue: 1,
    duration: 5000,
    useNativeDriver: true,
  }).start();
};
```
[2]

If you meant Angular, fade-in on entering a view uses `animate.enter` with a CSS class, e.g. `<div animate.enter="enter-animation">` inside an `@if` block, with the class defining a CSS `animation` that goes from `opacity: 0` to `opacity: 1`. [5]

Use `toSignal` from `@angular/core/rxjs-interop`. It creates a signal that tracks the value of an Observable, similar to the `async` pipe but usable anywhere in your app [3].

```ts
import {Component} from '@angular/core';
import {interval} from 'rxjs';
import {toSignal} from '@angular/core/rxjs-interop';

@Component({
  template: `{{ counter() }}`,
})
export class Ticker {
  counterObservable = interval(1000);

  // Get a `Signal` representing the `counterObservable`'s value.
  counter = toSignal(this.counterObservable, {initialValue: 0});
}
```
[3]

**Key points**

- **Subscription:** `toSignal` subscribes immediately, which may trigger side effects. It unsubscribes automatically when the component or service that called it is destroyed [3]. Avoid calling it repeatedly for the same Observable; reuse the returned signal [3].
- **Injection context:** It must run in an injection context, such as during construction of a component or service. Otherwise, pass an `Injector` manually [3].
- **Initial values:** Signals always need a current value. Options are:
  - `initialValue` sets the value returned before the Observable first emits [4].
  - If you provide neither, the signal returns `undefined` until the first emission [4].
  - `requireSync: true` is for Observables guaranteed to emit synchronously on subscription, like `BehaviorSubject`. The signal then always has a value, with no `undefined` type [4].
- **Errors and completion:** If the Observable errors, the error is thrown when the signal is read. If it completes, the signal keeps the last emitted value [1].
- **Cleanup:** Pass `manualCleanup` to override the automatic unsubscribe, which is useful for Observables that complete on their own [5].
- **Custom equality:** The `equal` option lets you define when consecutive values count as the same. Equal values don't update the signal [5].

Use the `loadComponent` property on the route (Angular router). It lazily loads the component's JavaScript when that route becomes active. [1]

```ts
import {Routes} from '@angular/router';

export const routes: Routes = [
  {
    path: 'login',
    loadComponent: () => import('./components/auth/login-page'),
  },
];
```
[1]

Key points:

- `loadComponent` takes a loader function that returns a Promise resolving to an Angular component. Usually this uses the standard dynamic `import()`, but any async loader function works. [1]
- If the lazily loaded file uses a `default` export, you can return the `import()` promise directly, without a `.then` to pick the exported class. [1]
- To lazy load child routes instead, use `loadChildren`, which loads them during route matching. [1]
- The loader runs in the route's injection context, so you can call `inject()` inside it, for example to choose which component to load based on a feature flag. [3]

```ts
loadComponent: () => {
  const flags = inject(FeatureFlags);
  return flags.isPremium
    ? import('./dashboard/premium-dashboard')
    : import('./dashboard/basic-dashboard');
},
```
[3]

Lazy loading moves the component code into separate chunks that are requested only when the user visits the route, which reduces the initial bundle size. [1] In general, eagerly load your primary landing page(s) and lazy load the others. [5]

The question doesn't name a framework, so here are the approaches the docs provide for Next.js and Angular.

## Next.js

**Option 1: Check the session in the page and call `unauthorized()`.** This renders your `unauthorized.tsx` file, for example with a login UI. [5]

```tsx
// app/dashboard/page.tsx
import { verifySession } from '@/app/lib/dal'
import { unauthorized } from 'next/navigation'

export default async function DashboardPage() {
  const session = await verifySession()

  if (!session) {
    unauthorized()
  }

  return <div>Dashboard</div>
}
```

```tsx
// app/unauthorized.tsx
import Login from '@/app/components/Login'

export default function UnauthorizedPage() {
  return (
    <main>
      <h1>401 - Unauthorized</h1>
      <p>Please log in to access this page.</p>
      <Login />
    </main>
  )
}
```
[5]

**Option 2: Protect Route Handlers the same way.** Calling `unauthorized()` there ensures only authenticated users can access the endpoint. [1]

**Option 3: Redirect with Proxy (optimistic check).** Proxy can centralize redirect logic and pre-filter unauthenticated users. It runs on every route, including prefetched ones, so only read the session from the cookie and avoid database checks. [4]

```tsx
// proxy.ts
import { NextRequest, NextResponse } from 'next/server'
import { decrypt } from '@/app/lib/session'
import { cookies } from 'next/headers'

const protectedRoutes = ['/dashboard']

export default async function proxy(req: NextRequest) {
  const path = req.nextUrl.pathname
  const isProtectedRoute = protectedRoutes.includes(path)

  const cookie = (await cookies()).get('session')?.value
  const session = await decrypt(cookie)

  if (isProtectedRoute && !session?.userId) {
    return NextResponse.redirect(new URL('/login', req.nextUrl))
  }

  return NextResponse.next()
}
```
[4]

## Angular

Use a route guard that returns `true` for authenticated users and otherwise redirects to `/login`: [2]

```ts
import {inject} from '@angular/core';
import {CanActivateFn, Router} from '@angular/router';
import {AuthStore} from './auth-store';

export const authGuard: CanActivateFn = () => {
  const authStore = inject(AuthStore);
  const router = inject(Router);
  return authStore.isAuthenticated() ? true : router.parseUrl('/login');
};
```
[2]

