Add `provideHttpClient` to the `providers` array in `app.config.ts`:

```ts
export const appConfig: ApplicationConfig = {
  providers: [provideHttpClient(/* add features here, such as withInterceptors(...) */)],
};
```
[2]

Then inject `HttpClient` where you need it:

```ts
@Service()
export class ConfigService {
  private http = inject(HttpClient);
  // This service can now make HTTP requests via `this.http`.
}
```
[2]

**Notes**

- In Angular v21 and later, `HttpClient` is available for injection by default. [1]
- `provideHttpClient` accepts optional features, such as `withInterceptors(...)` for request interceptors and `withXhr()` to switch from the default `fetch` backend to `XMLHttpRequest`. [4]
- Don't use `withXhr()` in SSR environments. XHR support on the server is deprecated, and the docs recommend the default `fetch` backend instead. [4]
- `HttpClientModule` is deprecated, so prefer `provideHttpClient` with the corresponding features. [3]

Use the `@for` block. It loops through a collection and renders its content once per item. The collection can be any JavaScript iterable, though Angular has extra performance optimizations for `Array` values [2].

```angular-html
@for (item of items; track item.id) {
  {{ item.name }}
}
```
[2]

**Key points**

- **`track` is required in the syntax shown.** It lets Angular maintain the relationship between your data and DOM nodes, so it performs the minimum DOM operations when data changes [2]. Prefer a unique property such as `id` or `uuid` [2].
- **Static collections:** you can track by `$index` [2].
- **Last resort:** `track item` tracks by reference identity (`===`), but it can make rendering updates significantly slower, so avoid it when possible [2].

**Empty fallback**

Add an `@empty` block right after the `@for` content to render something when there are no items:

```angular-html
@for (item of items; track item.name) {
  <li>{{ item.name }}</li>
} @empty {
  <li>There are no items.</li>
}
```
[3]

**Contextual variables**

Inside the block you have `$count`, `$index`, `$first`, `$last`, `$even`, and `$odd`. You can alias them with `let`, which is useful for nested `@for` blocks [1]:

```angular-html
@for (item of items; track item.id; let idx = $index, e = $even) {
  <p>Item #{{ idx }}: {{ item.name }}</p>
}
```
[1]

**Limitation:** `@for` doesn't support `continue` or `break` [2].

Yes, in Angular. The `@defer` block has a `viewport` trigger that loads the deferred content when specified content enters the viewport [3]. Wrapping a section of your template in `@defer` splits the code for its components, directives, and pipes into a separate JavaScript file that loads only when necessary [5].

```angular-html
@defer (on viewport) {
  <large-cmp />
} @placeholder {
  <div>Large component placeholder</div>
}
```

The `on viewport` syntax follows the same `on` pattern the docs show for other triggers [3]. The passages provided don't include a `viewport` example, so check the docs for how to specify which element to watch.

- **Prefetching:** You can add a prefetch trigger, separated from the main trigger by a semicolon, to load the JavaScript earlier. For example, `@defer (on interaction; prefetch on idle)` [4].
- **Layout shifts:** Don't defer components that are visible in the viewport on initial load, since that can increase cumulative layout shift (CLS). If you must, avoid `immediate`, `timer`, `viewport`, and custom `when` triggers that load content during the initial render [1].

**React Native:** The docs provided describe lazy-loading with React's `lazy` API, which defers loading a component's code until it's first rendered [2]. They don't describe a scroll-into-view trigger for it, so I can't say how to do that in React Native.

Use `input.required` instead of `input` when declaring the input:

```ts
@Component(/* ... */)
export class CustomSlider {
  // Declare a required input named value. Returns an `InputSignal<number>`.
  value = input.required<number>();
}
```
[1]

Angular enforces that required inputs must be set wherever the component is used in a template. If you leave one out, Angular reports a build-time error. [1]

Required inputs don't automatically include `undefined` in the generic parameter of the returned `InputSignal`. [1]

If you read a required input before its value is set, you get error NG0950. To avoid it, access the input in a reactive context (the template, a `computed`, or an `effect`), or read it in `ngOnInit` or later. [3]

In Angular, a child component sends an event to its parent through an output. The child calls `emit` on the output, optionally passing event data, and the parent listens in its template. [1]

**Child: emit the event**

```ts
// You can emit primitive values.
this.valueChanged.emit(7);

// You can emit custom event objects
this.thumbDropped.emit({
  pointerX: 123,
  pointerY: 456,
});
```
[1]

**Parent: listen in the template**

The event data is available through the `$event` variable:

```angular-html
<custom-slider (valueChanged)="logValue($event)" />
```
[1]

**Parent: receive the data in the component class**

```ts
@Component({
 /*...*/
})
export class App {
  logValue(value: number) {
    ...
  }
}
```
[1]

In tests, the output (an `OutputEmitterRef`) can be subscribed to directly with `subscribe`, which notifies subscribers synchronously. This mimics what the host component does implicitly. [5]

The docs provided don't show how the output is declared in the child class. They also don't cover this pattern for React Native or Next.js. The Next.js passage instead passes a bound Server Function down as a prop and uses a CSS attribute so an ancestor can react to child state. [4]

Provide it at the root level. Services are provisioned at the root by default, which gives you a single shared instance for the whole application, global availability without manual provider registration, and tree-shakability [2].

**Option 1: `@Service`**

`@Service` is a shorthand for `@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })` and implicitly provides a root singleton [2].

```ts
import {Service} from '@angular/core';

@Service()
export class UserClient {
  // Single instance shared across all components
}
```
[1]

**Option 2: `@Injectable({providedIn: 'root'})`**

Use this if you need constructor-based DI, advanced provider keys like `useClass`, or non-root scopes, which `@Service` doesn't support [2].

**Option 3: Provide it at application bootstrap**

Register it in `bootstrapApplication` providers when you want a true singleton, or when the provider has side effects or needs configuration [4].

```ts
bootstrapApplication(App, {
  providers: [
    LoggingService, // Used throughout the app
  ],
});
```
[4]

Bootstrap-provided services are always included in your bundle, even if never injected [4].

**Avoid** listing the service in a component's `providers` array. That creates a new instance per component instance, so state isn't shared [1][3].

Add the built-in `Validators.required` validator to the control. Import `Validators` from `@angular/forms`:

```ts
import {Validators} from '@angular/forms';
```

Then pass it as the second item in the control's array:

```ts
private formBuilder = inject(FormBuilder);

profileForm = this.formBuilder.group({
  firstName: ['', Validators.required],
  lastName: [''],
  address: this.formBuilder.group({
    street: [''],
    city: [''],
    state: [''],
    zip: [''],
  }),
});
```
[2]

A required control starts out invalid, and that status propagates to the parent form group. You can read it via the group's `status` property, for example `{{ profileForm.status }}` in the template. [2]

To show an error message, check the control's errors in the template:

```html
@if (name.invalid && (name.dirty || name.touched)) {
  @if (name.hasError('required')) {
    <div>Name is required.</div>
  }
}
```
[3]

Keep the `required` attribute on the input as well. It isn't needed for validation, but it's recommended for accessibility. [3]

```html
<input type="text" id="name" class="form-control" formControlName="name" required />
```
[3]

The context covers this for Angular only (the Next.js passage defines hydration but gives no enable steps).

**Prerequisite:** Hydration works only for server-side rendered apps, so enable SSR first. [1]

## Using Angular CLI

If you enabled SSR through the CLI (at app creation or later with `ng add @angular/ssr`), the code that enables hydration should already be in your app. [1]

## Manual setup

For a custom setup, import `provideClientHydration` from `@angular/platform-browser` and add it to your bootstrapping providers. [1]

Standalone bootstrap:

```typescript
import {
  bootstrapApplication,
  provideClientHydration,
} from '@angular/platform-browser';
...

bootstrapApplication(App, {
  providers: [provideClientHydration()]
});
```
[1]

With NgModules, add it to the root module's providers:

```typescript
import {provideClientHydration} from '@angular/platform-browser';
import {NgModule} from '@angular/core';

@NgModule({
  declarations: [App],
  exports: [App],
  bootstrap: [App],
  providers: [provideClientHydration()],
})
export class AppModule {}
```
[1]

## Server bootstrap

Make sure `provideClientHydration()` is also in the providers used to bootstrap the app on the **server**. With the default `ng new` structure, adding it to the root `AppModule` is enough because the server module imports it. With a custom setup, add it to the providers list in the server bootstrap configuration. [1][2]

**Not covered:** The docs provided don't include steps for enabling hydration in Next.js, which only describes hydration as React attaching event handlers to server-rendered HTML. [4]
